Allow the USB audio gadget to run a two-period buffer

u_audio.c advertises periods_min = MIN_PERIODS to ALSA, and MIN_PERIODS
is the constant 4 with no hardware justification attached. The effect on
the appliance, measured by asking for a buffer and then reading
hw_params back with the device open:

    asked period 32  periods 2  ->  period_size 32   buffer_size 128
    asked period 32  periods 3  ->  period_size 32   buffer_size 128
    asked period 64  periods 2  ->  period_size 64   buffer_size 256
    asked period 128 periods 2  ->  period_size 128  buffer_size 512

The buffer is always four periods whatever is requested, so the
board-side output buffer at a 32-frame quantum is 128 frames - 2.9ms -
when double buffering would need 64.

The one real constraint in that file is a few lines below:

    runtime->hw.period_bytes_min = 2 * prm->max_psize
                                    / runtime->hw.periods_min;

which keeps period_bytes_min * periods_min at 2 * max_psize - the
buffer must hold two USB packets, which is what double buffering means
and is preserved exactly by this change. For this configuration (22
channels, 24-bit, 44.1kHz, bInterval 1) max_psize is about 396 bytes,
so that floor is roughly three frames: far below the 32-frame period in
use, which is why periods_min rather than packet size is what binds.

Halving MIN_PERIODS therefore halves the minimum buffer without
weakening the packet-level guarantee.

--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_audio.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/function/u_audio.c
@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@
 
 #define BUFF_SIZE_MAX	(PAGE_SIZE * 16)
 #define PRD_SIZE_MAX	PAGE_SIZE
-#define MIN_PERIODS	4
+#define MIN_PERIODS	2
 
 enum {
 	UAC_FBACK_CTRL,
